1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,879
Here is our interoperability playground.

2
00:00:02,879 --> 00:00:10,939
I choose the standalone mock issuer and verifier, with the reference wallet as
holder.

3
00:00:10,939 --> 00:00:19,000
Starting the flow creates a new run ID and executes issuance, presentation and
verification.

4
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:21,667
The valid control completes successfully.

5
00:00:21,667 --> 00:00:29,667
I play the sequence and open a message to inspect its sanitized request and
response.

6
00:00:29,667 --> 00:00:35,000
Each exchange belongs to a named step in this run.

7
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:38,138
Now I change the signature and repeat.

8
00:00:38,138 --> 00:00:39,931
The verifier returns Invalid.

9
00:00:39,931 --> 00:00:43,517
That is the expected outcome for this fault.

10
00:00:43,517 --> 00:00:48,000
Both results come from the services running during this recording.

11
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:53,885
I compare the two runs and open the matrix.

12
00:00:53,885 --> 00:00:57,808
The comparison preserves configuration and outcomes.

13
00:00:57,808 --> 00:01:05,000
Reports and matrix exports provide a reviewable record of each combination.

14
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:17,931
Here are the saved real-wallet observations, the findings explorer, the party
registry and QR capabilities.

15
00:01:17,931 --> 00:01:23,103
The registry describes formats and flows.

16
00:01:23,103 --> 00:01:30,000
The capabilities page distinguishes measured support from claims.

17
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:39,000
The Android guide explains the phone journey and its network requirements.

18
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:48,000
For the local mock prototype, run pnpm demo:docker and open localhost:5174.

19
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:53,850
Next is the assisted JSON-LD fixture.

20
00:01:53,850 --> 00:02:06,525
I start a connected run, simulate issuance, confirm storage, and submit the
presentation.

21
00:02:06,525 --> 00:02:10,425
The result is Valid.

22
00:02:10,425 --> 00:02:18,225
After reloading, the saved run is still available.

23
00:02:18,225 --> 00:02:27,000
These controls demonstrate the assisted workflow using fixture data.

24
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:32,143
This is the recorded local Inji Web card after reload.

25
00:02:32,143 --> 00:02:39,857
The same wallet received the JSON-LD credential from Certify and presented it to
local Verify.

26
00:02:39,857 --> 00:02:45,000
The setup used synthetic authorization and an explicit DID adapter.

27
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:51,207
The managed local inspector links captured exchanges to the run.

28
00:02:51,207 --> 00:02:56,793
It exposes permitted field names while withholding credential values.

29
00:02:56,793 --> 00:03:03,000
Browser transport and Mimoto server calls have distinct capture coverage.

30
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:10,448
The hosted Web journey has its own recorded run and valid verification.

31
00:03:10,448 --> 00:03:16,655
Its environment and timestamps remain separate from the local journey.

32
00:03:16,655 --> 00:03:21,000
Historical records can be opened for review.

33
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:27,429
The physical Android record used local Certify and hosted Verify.

34
00:03:27,429 --> 00:03:34,500
The operator stored and reopened the card, then presented it successfully.

35
00:03:34,500 --> 00:03:39,000
A managed all-local Android run remains open.

36
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:45,471
This comparison isolates issuer key location across two Verify versions.

37
00:03:45,471 --> 00:03:53,235
ES256 and RS256 passed with a matching certificate and failed without one.

38
00:03:53,235 --> 00:04:01,000
Supplying the native walt.id signer’s certificate also restored Valid on both
versions.

39
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:16,146
Our evidence table covers authorization code with PKCE, pre-authorized issuance,
cross-vendor discovery adapters, mDL signature and digest checks, and the browser
credential chooser.

40
00:04:16,146 --> 00:04:23,390
The matrix indexes 79 observations and the register tracks 15 findings.

41
00:04:23,390 --> 00:04:28,000
Each claim links its evidence and limits.

42
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:34,923
The sample-data index gives synthetic inputs, instructions and expected outcomes.

43
00:04:34,923 --> 00:04:39,077
The API reference documents the backend.

44
00:04:39,077 --> 00:04:46,000
Test evidence and dated reports record the checks actually executed.

45
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:50,118
The readiness page records completed checks and pending human tasks.

46
00:04:50,118 --> 00:05:00,000
Reviewers can run the Docker prototype or inspect the public evidence archive, then
follow a result back to its version, run and sanitized exchanges.
